  "Question": "Given \\(12\\) points in a plane no three of which are collinear, the number of lines they determine is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(24\\)",
    "B": "\\(54\\)",
    "C": "\\(120\\)",
    "D": "\\(66\\)",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Two distinct points determine exactly one line. Since no three of the given points are collinear, each determined line contains exactly two of them. Thus different unordered pairs give different lines, and every determined line is included by counting pairs.\n\nThere are \\(12\\) choices for a first point and \\(11\\) choices for a second point. This counts each pair twice, once in each order, so the number of lines is\n\\[\n\\frac{12\\cdot11}{2}=\\boxed{66}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice D.",
